---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Projects, Tenants & Domains">
  <h1>Projects, Tenants &amp; Domains</h1>
  <p>
    Identity separates three concepts that older versions conflated into a
    single "tenant". Getting them straight is the key to the whole isolation
    model:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Project</strong> — the isolation boundary. Think of it as a
      Firebase project: one user pool, one set of credentials, one JWT issuer.
      Each Project maps onto exactly one storage scope (one shard).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tenant</strong> — a logical company or team entity
      <em>inside</em> a Project. Tenants form automatically from the email
      domains users sign up with (e.g. <code>acme.com</code>), or a signed-in
      user creates one where the Project allows
      <a href="#self-service-tenants">self-service tenants</a>. A single
      Project can hold many Tenants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Domain</strong> — a DNS domain a Tenant registers and proves
      it controls. Verifying <code>acme.com</code> binds it to that one
      Tenant, so identity attributes <code>alice@acme.com</code> to it; until
      then the registration is only a claim, and several Tenants may hold
      one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Project is the isolation shard</h2>
  <p>
    The Project — not the tenant — is the unit of physical isolation. All
    per-request resolution starts by resolving the Project (the shard), either
    by API credential (<code>public_id</code> on a publishable / secret /
    mTLS credential) or by the <code>Host</code> header matching a configured
    project auth-domain. The user's Tenants are resolved <em>afterward</em>,
    from their memberships, and never select the Project. A token's
    <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    (<code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code>) unless the client
    <a href="#choosing-a-tenant-for-a-token">chooses a tenant</a> on refresh.
  </p>
  <p>
    This inversion means a single B2C product (millions of users, no company
    concept) and a B2B product (many company tenants) use the same model: one
    Project per product, Tenants grow underneath it without re-sharding.
  </p>

  <h2>The user pool is per Project; login is always by email</h2>
  <p>
    Users belong to a <strong>Project</strong>, not a Tenant. Uniqueness is
    <code>(project_id, lower(email))</code> — exactly one identity per person
    per Project, no matter how many company domains they touch. Authentication
    always resolves the identity by <code>(project_id, email)</code> first; the
    user's Tenants are derived afterward from their memberships, a domain one
    counting only while the email is verified and on a domain the tenant has
    verified. The Tenant is never an input to login, and signing in leaves the
    token's <code>tenant</code> claim at the deployment default.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# A user proves: "I am alice@acme.com in project P"
# identity then derives: "...and acme.com makes her a member of Acme"
PasswordLogin(project=P, email="alice@acme.com", password=...)
  -> identity   (project_id=P, email=alice@acme.com)
  -> membership (Acme, from the verified acme.com domain; see ListMyTenants)
  -> token      ("tenant": GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID until a refresh chooses Acme)`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2>Per-project serving domains</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can serve auth on one or more hostnames
    (<em>project auth-domains</em>). Incoming requests without an explicit
    credential are resolved to a Project by matching the <code>Host</code>
    header against <code>project_auth_domains.hostname</code>. One domain per
    project is marked primary (used to build links such as magic-link and
    OAuth callback URLs). The domain-management RPCs
    (<code>CreateDomain</code>, <code>VerifyDomain</code>,
    <code>AddProjectAuthDomain</code>, <code>SetPrimaryAuthDomain</code>) are
    documented in the API reference.
  </p>

  <h2>One Project per deployment, many on one datastore</h2>
  <p>
    A single Identity container serves one default Project, configured at
    startup. Multiple Projects can share one Postgres instance and stay fully
    isolated — every repository query is scoped by
    <code>WHERE project_id = $1</code>, which is the real isolation boundary and
    is backend-agnostic. On Postgres, row-level security adds defence-in-depth
    on top; the per-query project scope is what guarantees isolation on every
    backend.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# Two products sharing one Postgres, isolated by Project
# Product A
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_a \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0

# Product B
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_b \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2 id="self-service-tenants">Self-service tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can let its signed-in users create tenants of their own, for a
    team that has no company domain behind it. It is off by
    default; the Project turns it on in its <code>config_json</code> (the
    default Project takes it from
    <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_ENABLED</code> when a request falls
    through to it; one that reaches the default Project by its own project
    key or auth hostname reads that Project's <code>config_json</code>
    instead):
  </p>
  <Code code={`{ "tenants": { "self_service": true } }`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    <code>CreateTenant(name)</code> then creates a claimed tenant and makes the
    caller its <code>owner</code>, in one transaction. A caller who has not
    verified their email is refused with <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. Each user may create at
    most <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_MAX_PER_USER</code> tenants (default
    10) per Project in <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>
    (default one day); past that the call is refused with
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>. With the setting off it is
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. A retried call can create a second
    tenant, so reconcile with <code>ListMyTenants</code>, which returns every
    claimed tenant the caller is an active member of, with their role. A
    membership that came from the email's domain is listed only while that
    email is verified and on a domain the tenant has verified. A self-service
    tenant cannot be renamed or deleted yet, and a member cannot leave one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant admin registers and verifies email domains with
    <code>CreateDomain</code> and <code>VerifyDomain</code>. A tenant may make
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_MAX_PER_TENANT</code> of them
    together (default 100) in
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> (default one
    day), and both share a per-IP budget
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_DOMAIN_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window) on
    the HTTP surface: over it a call gets HTTP 429 with a
    <code>Retry-After</code> header, which Connect clients surface as
    <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. A host serving identity over native gRPC
    applies its own. A registered domain captures no signups until it is
    verified, and a failed re-check never un-verifies it. Registering a
    domain another tenant has verified is <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code>, so a
    tenant admin can learn which domains are onboarded in the Project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant is administered only while it is claimed or latent. Once an
    operator suspends it, its owners and admins are refused with
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> by every domain and membership call
    (registering, verifying and listing domains; inviting; listing
    invitations and members; removing a member; transferring ownership), and
    an invitation to it can no longer be accepted. Nothing they had set up is changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    An owner hands the tenant on with
    <code>TransferTenantOwnership(tenant_id, user_id)</code>: the member
    named becomes an owner and the caller an admin, in one step, so the
    tenant is never left without an owner. Naming a co-owner leaves them an
    owner and the caller an admin, which is how an owner steps down. Only an
    owner may do it (<code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> otherwise), to an active
    member (<code>NOT_FOUND</code> otherwise; a member through their email's
    domain only while the tenant verifies that domain) who could create a
    tenant themselves: an active, signed-up person with a verified email
    (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> otherwise). The last owner cannot be
    removed, even by two removals at once or a removal racing a transfer, so
    a tenant changes hands this way. The transfer is audited as
    <code>tenant_ownership_transferred</code>. A transfer is not idempotent:
    once it succeeds the caller is an admin, so a retry is
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>; a client that lost the response reads
    <code>ListTenantMembers</code> to see where ownership stands. An admin
    can still invite an owner, themselves included, so being the owner does
    not yet keep a tenant from its admins (tracked in
    <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/issues/657">issue #657,
    admins can make themselves owners</a>).
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing-a-tenant-for-a-token">Choosing a tenant for a token</h2>
  <p>
    An access token's <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    unless the client asks for another on refresh. Selection is off by
    default; a server turns it on with
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_SELECTION_ENABLED</code>, on the Postgres driver
    only:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`RefreshToken(refresh_token=..., tenant_id="3f9c2a7b5e1d4c8fa06b9e2d7c41f5a3")
  -> access token with "tenant": "3f9c2a7b..."  # an active member the tenant's login policy admits
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED, detail NOT_A_MEMBER     # not a member, or not claimed; token unspent
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED, detail LOGIN_POLICY     # the tenant's login policy refuses this session; token unspent
  -> UNAVAILABLE, detail UNAVAILABLE            # membership or policy unreadable; token unspent
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION, detail NOT_ENABLED    # selection is off on this server; token unspent
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT, no detail                # not a tenant id at all; token not read`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A member only through their email's domain must have verified that email
    and still be on a domain the tenant has verified; moving the email off the
    domain ends the membership, and moving back does not rejoin. Every refused
    selection carries a <code>TenantSelectionRefusedDetails</code> error
    detail and consumes nothing, so a client that finds the detail refreshes
    again without <code>tenant_id</code>. <code>RefreshToken</code> refuses an
    account over its own state with the same codes and no such detail, and
    that refusal may have spent the token, so retry only when the detail is
    present. A <code>tenant_id</code> longer than 64 characters, or holding
    anything but ASCII letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>, is
    <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> before the refresh token is read. Each refresh chooses again; one without
    <code>tenant_id</code> returns to the default, and the client reads the
    tenant it got from the token's <code>tenant</code> claim. Identity's HTTP
    surface checks the tenant's status through a short cache, so suspending a
    tenant refuses its tokens there within
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_STATUS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code> (default 30); ending
    a membership takes effect at the next
    refresh, within the access-token lifetime. That holds in
    <code>mode=session</code> too: removing a member ends none of their
    sessions, so a token that already carries the tenant stays valid until it
    expires. A service that verifies tokens
    itself, as a host serving identity over native gRPC does, must accept a
    non-default <code>tenant</code> before its clients select one, and sees a
    suspension only when the token expires unless it checks the tenant too.
  </p>
  <p>
    The claim asserts membership, and that the tenant's login policy admitted
    the session when the token was issued. <code>role</code> stays the
    account's role and is never a role in the chosen tenant (read that from
    <code>ListMyTenants</code>). A policy that limits how its users sign in
    (SSO required, 2FA required, or a list of allowed methods) admits only a
    session it governed at sign-in, one whose account's email is on a domain
    the tenant has verified. A session does not record how it signed in, so a
    member from another domain is refused with <code>LOGIN_POLICY</code>
    even if they did sign in through the tenant's SSO; the tenant can invite
    them on its own domain instead. The policy's session timeouts apply to
    every selection, and a session past them is refused the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Invitations</h2>
  <p>
    A tenant owner or admin invites by email with
    <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code>. Every invitation sends an email, so
    each is bounded: per client IP
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window),
    per tenant (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_TENANT</code>, default
    100, in <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>, default one
    day), per inviter across their tenants
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_INVITER</code>, default 100), per
    tenant and recipient
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_RECIPIENT</code>, default 10, each
    tenant counting its own), and per tenant and recipient by a resend cooldown
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code>, default
    300). Past any of these but the per-IP one the call is
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>; past the per-IP one, which applies on the
    HTTP surface only, it gets HTTP 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code>
    header, which Connect clients surface as <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. The email names an operator's or a domain
    tenant, but never a self-service tenant, whose name its creator typed.
    Accepting an invitation needs a verified email at the invited address
    (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> until then; the invitation stays open),
    and never lowers a role, so it cannot demote an owner.
  </p>

  <h2 id="operating-tenants">Operating tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A platform operator reads a Project's tenants with the control-plane
    admin calls, which authenticate with the <code>X-Admin-Secret</code>
    header (<code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code>) rather than a user token.
    They return the tenant's own record, never its members, domains or
    policy:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminGetTenant(project_id, tenant_id)
  -> { id, name, primary_domain, status, created_by, created_at, updated_at }
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT              # a blank project_id or tenant_id
  -> NOT_FOUND                     # the Project holds no such tenant
AdminListTenants(project_id, limit=50, cursor="")
  -> { tenants: [...], next_cursor }   # every status, newest first
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT              # a blank project_id, or a cursor that does not decode
Both:
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED                 # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED             # a wrong X-Admin-Secret`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    <code>limit</code> defaults to 50 and is capped at 200. Pass each page's
    <code>next_cursor</code> to read the next; it is empty on the last page.
    A cursor holds the last tenant's position, not an offset, so paging never
    repeats or skips a tenant that existed when it began; a tenant created
    meanwhile may or may not appear. A cursor is not bound to the Project it
    came from: the page is always of <code>project_id</code>'s tenants alone,
    and a Project with no tenants, or one that does not exist, is an empty
    page.
  </p>
  <p>
    An operator stops a tenant by suspending it, and starts it again by
    restoring it. Each call needs a reason, which the audit trail keeps as
    <code>tenant_suspended</code> or <code>tenant_restored</code>:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminSuspendTenant(project_id, tenant_id, reason="billing dispute")
  -> { tenant: { status: "suspended", ... } }  # was claimed, or already suspended
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION                       # latent: it governs nothing yet
AdminRestoreTenant(project_id, tenant_id, reason="dispute settled")
  -> { tenant: { status: "claimed", ... } }    # was suspended, or already claimed
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION                       # latent, or no creator, owner or admin:
                                               #   add an admin with AdminAddTenantAdmin first
Both:
  -> ABORTED                                   # its status changed meanwhile; read it again
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT                          # a blank id, or a reason that is blank, over 500
                                               #   characters, or holds a control or formatting
                                               #   character such as a newline
  -> NOT_FOUND                                 # the Project holds no such tenant
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED                             # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED                         # a wrong X-Admin-Secret`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A suspended tenant keeps its domains, members and invitations. A refresh
    that chooses it is refused, its selected tokens are refused within the
    tenant-status cache TTL (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_STATUS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code>,
    30 seconds by default), and its owners and admins can no longer
    administer it. The agent grants and machines of its members, in any
    membership status, and of the accounts on its verified domains and its
    primary domain are revoked.
  </p>
  <p>
    While it is suspended its login policy does not govern sign-in: the
    accounts on its verified domains sign in under the Project's default
    controls, so its allowed methods, SSO and 2FA requirements, password
    minimum and session timeouts do not apply to them. Where its policy is
    stricter than the Project's, a suspension loosens the controls on those
    accounts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Restoring it gives back administration at once and selection within the
    same cache TTL, and brings back its login policy. Like any change of a
    tenant's status, it revokes again the agent grants and machines of the
    same members and accounts, including any connected during the
    suspension; no grant comes back. Asking for the
    status a tenant already has returns it unchanged and records nothing, so a
    retry is safe; only the call that changes the status records its reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant whose owners have all been deleted, deactivated or merged away
    has no owner. While one of its admins can sign in, that admin invites a
    new owner with <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code> and
    <code>role="owner"</code>. Once no owner or admin can sign in, nobody can
    govern it, and an operator gives it an owner again, with a reason the
    audit trail keeps as <code>tenant_ownership_recovered</code>:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminRecoverTenantOwnership(project_id, tenant_id, user_id, reason="owner left the company")
  -> { membership: { role: "owner", source: "added", ... } }   # an existing invited or created source is kept
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # an owner or admin who is a person can still sign in,
                           #   the account cannot own a tenant, or latent
  -> NOT_FOUND             # no such tenant or account`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The account may be any person in the Project who could own a tenant,
    member or not, and a suspended tenant can be recovered. While an owner
    or an admin who is a person can still sign in, recovery is refused: an
    owner transfers, an admin invites. So recovery never puts an outside
    account above people who can act. An account pending deletion can still
    sign in, which cancels the deletion, so an owner or admin in that state
    still counts. Naming an account that is already an owner who can sign in
    returns its membership and records nothing, so a retry is safe. A
    refused recovery is not audited. <code>AdminAddTenantAdmin</code>, which
    provisions a tenant's first admins, makes none of these checks and
    records nothing, so the same admin secret can make anyone an owner or
    admin of any tenant, lowering an owner included.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recovery treats a deactivated or suspended owner as gone, though either
    can be undone: an owner reactivated after a recovery is an owner again,
    beside the appointed one, so make sure the account will not come back
    first. When every owner is gone and the admins left cannot be reached
    or will not act, a Project admin deactivates their accounts with
    <code>DeactivateUser</code> (audited, and reversible with
    <code>ReactivateUser</code>, but it signs each account out of every
    tenant) and the operator then recovers the tenant.
  </p>

  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/auth-model">Auth model</a> for how the Project
    and Tenant appear in JWT claims, and
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration">Configuration</a> for the full
    set of <code>GATEWAY_*</code> variables.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
